  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1153 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA rex block will take shape over the southeast CONUS over the next \n24 hours or so, as an upper low forms over the Florida panhandle \nwhile a ridge builds to its north over the Appalachians. The rex \nblock pattern weakens/shifts east on Thursday due to a trough moving \nthrough the upper midwest, however H85 ridging will remain anchored \noff the Carolina coastline through Fri with upper heights making a \nbit of a rebound on Fri as well. All of this to say that this \npattern should keep dry conditions in place through the end of the \nweek, with gradually warming temperatures to go along with it. \nTemperatures will wind up in the realm of 8-10 degrees above normal \nby Friday.\n\nAlso of note will be low afternoon RH today and on Thursday to some \ndegree as well. NBM is once again too high with afternoon dewpoints \nI believe, so a blend of 00Z HRRR as well as some CONSShort \nguidance, brought dewpoints down into the upper 20s to low 30s this \nafternoon with resulting afternoon RH values dropping into the low \n20s to around 30 percent. For Thursday it's not quite as bad as \nmodel guidance does show PWATs increasing slightly due to persistent \neast-southeasterly H85 flow bringing a very minor increase in low \nlevel moisture into play. Suspect the most notable increase, \nrelatively speaking, in moisture will be the southern tier of \ncounties, especially our NC counties while northern areas once again \nsee fairly low afternoon RH values. Used a similar blend as \nWednesday but didn't go quite as far with it as I'm not sure how dry \nit will be Thu afternoon.\n\nBy the weekend, models are generally converging on a scenario where \none upper wave/weak low pressure system tracks up the coast of the \nCarolinas on Saturday while another short wave and weak cold front \napproach from the west on Sunday, moving across our area sometime \nSunday night into Monday morning. Trends have been for a further \neastward track of the coastal system, meaning the dry conditions \nlikely continue into the first half of the weekend. NBM has low rain \nchances, mainly for the northern half of the forecast area, Sunday \nnight into Mon morning with the next system which seems reasonable \nfor now.\n\nMonday and Tuesday look dry behind the front, with temperatures \nback down closer to seasonal normals.",
